rticle-title>Sea otters their role in structuring nearshore communities.</article-title><source>Science</source><volume>185</volume><fpage>1058</fpage><lpage>1060</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000295-Alpine1"><label>27</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Alpine</surname><given-names>AE</given-names></name><name><surname>Cloern</surname><given-names>JE</given-names></name></person-group><year>1992</year><article-title>Trophic interactions and direct physical effects control phytoplankton biomass and production in an estuary.</article-title><source>Limnology &#x00026; Oceanography</source><volume>37</volume><fpage>946</fpage><lpage>955</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000295-McCann1"><label>28</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>McCann</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Hastings</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Huxel</surname><given-names>GR</given-names></name></person-group><year>1998</year><article-title>Weak trophic interactions and the balance of nature.</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>395</volume><fpage>794</fpage><lpage>798</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000295-Berlow1"><label>29</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Berlow</surname><given-names>EL</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>Strong effects of weak interactions in ecological communities.</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>398</volume><fpage>330</fpage><lpage>334</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000295-Gamfeldt1"><label>30</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gamfeldt</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Hillebrand</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Jonsson</surname><given-names>PR</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Species richness changes across two trophic levels simultaneously affect prey and consumer biomass.</article-title><source>Ecology Letters</source><volume>8</volume><fpage>696</fpage><lpage>703</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000295-Thebault1"><label>31</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Thebault</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Loreau</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>The relationship between biodiversity and ecosystem functioning in food webs.</article-title><source>Ecological Research</source><volume>21</volume><fpage>17</fpage><lpage>25</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000295-Duffy3"><label>32</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Duffy</surname><given-names>JE</given-names></name><name><surname>Richardson</surname><given-names>JP</given-names></name><name><surname>Canuel</surname><given-names>EA</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Grazer diversity effects on ecosystem functioning in seagrass beds.</article-title><source>Ecology Letters</source><volume>6</volume><fpage>637</fpage><lpage>645</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000295-Duffy4"><label>33</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Duffy</surname><given-names>JE</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Biodiversity and the functioning of seagrass ecosystems.</article-title><source>Marine Ecology-Progress Series</source><volume>311</volume><fpage>233</fpage><lpage>250</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000295-Levin1"><label>34</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Levin</surname><given-names>PS</given-names></name><name><surname>Coyer</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><name><surname>Petrik</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Good</surname><given-names>TP</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Community-wide effects of nonindigenous species on temperate rocky reefs.</article-title><source>Ecology</source><volume>83</volume><fpage>3182</fpage><lpage>3193</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000295-Steneck1"><label>35</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Steneck</surname><given-names>RS</given-names></name><name><surname>Vavrinec</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Leland</surname><given-names>AV</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Accelerating trophic-level dysfunction in kelp forest ecosystems of the western North Atlantic.</article-title><source>Ecosystems</source><volume>7</volume><fpage>323</fpage><lpage>332</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000295-Parker1"><label>36</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Parker</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name><name><surname>Burkepile</surname><given-names>DE</given-names></name><name><surname>Hay</surname><given-names>ME</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Opposing effects of native and exotic herbivores on plant invasions.</article-title><source>Science</source><volume>311</volume><fpage>1459</fpage><lpage>1461</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16527979</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000295-Lotze2"><label>37</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lotze</surname><given-names>HK</given-names></name><name><surname>Reise</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Worm</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>van Beusekom</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Busch</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Human transformations of the Wadden Sea ecosystem through time: a synthesis.</article-title><source>Helgoland Marine Research</source><volume>59</volume><fpage>84</fpage></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig id="pone-0000295-g001" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000295.g001</object-id><label>Figure 1</label><caption><title>Food web showing the connections between all trophic functional groups.</title><p>Arrows represent one group consuming the group to which the arrow points. Shading indicates trophic level (none&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1, light&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;2, moderate&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;3, dark&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;4).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000295.g001"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000295-g002" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000295.g002</object-id><label>Figure 2</label><caption><title>Trophic skew in regional invasions versus combined global and regional extinctions broken down by percentage of species in each trophic group.</title><p>Colors indicate trophic level (white&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1, light grey&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;2, dark grey&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;3, black&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;4). Extinctions are skewed towards trophic levels 3 and 4 (secondary consumers and predators) while invasions are skewed towards trophic level 2 (primary consumers).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000295.g002"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000295-g003" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000295.g003</object-id><label>Figure 3</label><caption><title>Trophic skew in the Wadden Sea in (A) uninvaded intact communities, (B) exotic species that have successfully established and (C) species that have gone extinct.</title><p>Data presented as the percentage of species in each trophic group. Colors indicate trophic level (white&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1, light grey&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;2, dark grey&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;3, black&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;4). Patterns match those in the larger global/regional data set (<xref ref-type="fig" rid="pone-0000295-g002">Fig. 2</xref>), with extinctions and invasions occurring within different trophic groups, and neither matching the natural trophic distribution of species.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000295.g003"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000295-g004" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000295.g004</object-id><label>Figure 4</label><caption><title>Trophic distribution of species in the Wadden Sea represented as trophic pyramids.</title><p>Bar widths are scaled to the proportion of species in each trophic level and numbers indicate percentage of species loss or gain in each trophic level. Colors indicate trophic level (white&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1, light grey&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;2, dark grey&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;3, black&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;4). (A) Pre-Disturbance (B) Skew of extinctions (C) Skew of Invasions (D) Current distribution after invasions and extinctions resulting in 5.1&#x00025; community turnover (i.e., equal numbers of extinctions and invasions) (E) Projected change in the trophic distribution of species in the Wadden Sea for 25&#x00025; community turnover. Note the dramatic reduction of the proportion of species in the top two trophic levels, and the increasing dominance of primary consumers.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000295.g004"/></fig></sec><fn-group><fn fn-type="conflict"><p><bold>Competing Interests: </bold>The authors have declared that no competing interests exist.</p></fn><fn fn-type="financial-disclosure"><p><bold>Funding: </bold>Funding and other support for this study was provided by the UC Davis IGERT on Biological Invasions (NSF-DGE No. 0114432) and the National Science Foundation Biological Oceanography program (OCE 03-17778 to JJS).</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>